Managed worker abstractions make lifecycle policy visible. A task that escapes its owner also escapes that owner’s shutdown and failure handling.

> Elixir can’t manage what it doesn’t know about.

Source: [Q04](/lore/durability-bibliography#q04); [B3](/lore/durability-bibliography#b3-designing-elixir-systems-with-otp).

## Failure it prevents

A request returns but unobserved child work fails or holds resources indefinitely.

## Our Effect 4.0.0 reading

`Effect.forkScoped` and `FiberSet` attach work to a [Scope](/lore/scopes-own-resources). `Effect.forkDetach` detaches the [fiber](/lore/concurrent-work-needs-an-owner) from its parent. The runtime can still stop it. Add recovery policy separately.

Source: `packages/effect/src/Effect.ts`, `packages/effect/src/Scope.ts`, `packages/effect/src/FiberSet.ts`.

## How to check

Interrupt the owner. Verify child completion or interruption, resource release, and the reported outcome. This check is our proposal. The books do not prescribe it.

Continue with [Supervision chooses what restarts](/lore/supervision-is-a-recovery-policy), [Durable work needs storage](/lore/durable-work-needs-storage).

